00:01:53.195 --> 00:01:57.090
The idea we have is pretty much
you want to get a certain

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.120
sets of cards.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.838
And we get sets by drawing cards
or trading with other

00:02:02.838 --> 00:02:04.220
people or--

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.950
but it's also you can take cards
if you want, but if you

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:09.342
get caught, then it's up to the
other players whether you

00:02:09.342 --> 00:02:10.370
get punished or not.

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:11.750
PROFESSOR: OK, if you
get caught then--

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:12.734
OK.

00:02:12.734 --> 00:02:13.984
So [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:02:18.935 --> 00:02:19.724
That sounds good.

00:02:19.724 --> 00:02:22.194
So it sounds like we
have four teams.

00:02:22.194 --> 00:02:23.444
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:31.800
So today I'm planning on, for
the most part, just going

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.860
through the lecture stuff that
I had planned for today and

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.260
for last Wednesday.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.610
And if there's any time left,
we could finish up with

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:43.860
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.840
And that's what I'd
like to do.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:50.830
But if there's only like an hour
left, then I think I'd

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.015
probably rather you play
one more game.

00:02:54.015 --> 00:02:57.540
As this is a useful exercise,
but playing these games are

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:02.770
just as useful and frankly
a better design.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.470
All right.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:05.756
Let's see.

00:03:05.756 --> 00:03:08.510
So I think I'll start with
Wednesday's stuff first, which

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.220
is the last [COLERA]

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.920
reading for the entire
semester.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.720
I did, in fact, actually look
a little bit into the weird

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:21.612
eskimo stuff from Monday, and
one thing, I don't actually

00:03:21.612 --> 00:03:22.760
think he was speaking
figuratively.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.800
I think he was actually
describing something that he

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:32.254
had done some anthropoligical
research on-- this is a ritual

00:03:32.254 --> 00:03:36.615
that a very, very
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], just a fine

00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:39.430
group of eskimos do.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.656
It reminds me a lot of the sort
of recitations that you

00:03:42.656 --> 00:03:45.260
get that sometimes has
a morbid quality,

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:45.880
sometimes they don't.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.270
But this reminds you of either
historical events, or

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:50.220
important things to do.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.770
Like the easiest one that
I keep in my brain is

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.970
righty-tighty, lefty-loosey.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:58.590
That teaches you something
of how screws work.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.894
But then they're a little bit
like ring around the rosy,

00:04:00.894 --> 00:04:01.870
with a pocket full
of poseys, ashes,

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:03.425
ashes, we all fall down.

00:04:03.425 --> 00:04:06.850
But it's actually a
commemorative little poem

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:11.138
about the black death.

00:04:11.138 --> 00:04:14.010
That's what that
song is about.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.029
So you have these
little things.

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:20.920
That particular example that
he was describing was more

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:25.070
about this recitation that you
sort of remember the procedure

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.465
in which things are supposed
to be done.

00:04:28.465 --> 00:04:30.250
But they're [UNINTELLIGIBLE],
they're sort of

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:30.940
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.754
reenactment about what you
do in order to I guess

00:04:34.754 --> 00:04:36.185
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:04:36.185 --> 00:04:40.540
or something like that
to basically perform

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:43.682
taxodermy on an animal.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:50.070
One of those ways where a
playful experience was pretty

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:54.646
much it's been used as
a learning tool.

00:04:54.646 --> 00:04:58.920
And that kind of gets back to
something I was mentioning

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:00.460
much earlier in this course.

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:04.600
In many ways, learning
how to play a game is

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.730
literally very exotic.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:09.116
You can't figure out how to play
the game unless you have

00:05:09.116 --> 00:05:10.880
figured out all the rules.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.555
And if the rules happen to have
some sort of correlation

00:05:12.555 --> 00:05:14.956
to something real world, you
might end up learning

00:05:14.956 --> 00:05:16.100
something about that
real world.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.860
Whether you transfer to back
to actually something real

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:24.690
world is kind of actually the
topic of what Wednesday's

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.855
reading about.

00:05:27.855 --> 00:05:28.370
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:33.160
talks about mimicry and things
being kind of taken over by

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.180
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.360
Now he uses these grand,
sweeping statements which I

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.580
think sound a lot more modest
when they're in the original

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.360
language, and what to translate
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.210
sound like you're saying, and
this huge wave of competitive

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.418
games took over witch doctors.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:56.980
But I think what he's referring
to, and if we notice

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:58.760
the readings for that chapter
three and chapter eight.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.936
So there's a whole bunch of
stuff that's missing, and we

00:06:01.936 --> 00:06:05.040
kind of wanted to spare you
from the bad translation.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:13.010
But the idea is that there is a
lot of stuff that basically

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:17.820
mimicry, the old process of
putting on masks, putting on

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:22.680
costumes, and being someone else
has great societal power.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:27.110
This had great societal power.

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:31.370
And this is more of an
anthropological thing.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:34.420
But the idea being you have
these people-- who are

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:36.510
effectively leaders
of groups--

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.470
shamans, witch doctors, fortune

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:41.410
tellers, maybe just elders.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:44.530
And they would have costumes,
they'd have masks that not

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:51.600
only gave them station, what
when I wear this robe I am now

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.450
in charge of this tribe,
but also turned them

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.760
into somebody else.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.372
Now, if you look at ancient
accounts of the Egyptian

00:07:02.372 --> 00:07:05.300
pharoahs, for instance, in
order to actually give up

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:08.270
their own name and then they
take on a new name.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:09.596
And a lot of them actually just
become Pharaoh in the

00:07:09.596 --> 00:07:15.530
same way that a lot of Roman
emperors just become Caesar.

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:18.000
You can think of Caesear
as just being king.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:21.952
So it becomes a title, but
you also become a person.

00:07:21.952 --> 00:07:23.928
A lot of the--

00:07:23.928 --> 00:07:24.916
I'm trying to remember--

00:07:24.916 --> 00:07:31.820
Persian, I think, emperors
become Darius or Cyrus.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.290
Kind of just one word.

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:36.230
This is one name that was
given to the king.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:39.180
That's who you become.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:45.990
And that kind of combined with
aesthetic solemn rituals.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:48.683
A lot of these are not what
you would recognize

00:07:48.683 --> 00:07:51.350
as games right now.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:55.250
But in many ways they sort of
tap into the same sort of

00:07:55.250 --> 00:08:00.150
pleasures of sort of critical
and losing control.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:04.120
Once in a while, you don't
have to be sort of all

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.120
inhibited and obeying laws.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.620
You can just cut loose.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:12.429
And in superstitious tribes,

00:08:12.429 --> 00:08:15.510
superstitition will be the excuse.

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:19.460
But you don't actually
necessarily need that reason.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.750
And I think one thing that
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:25.790
sort of describes in chapter
eight is how--

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:28.195
we kind of get that today too.

00:08:28.195 --> 00:08:33.260
He was describing stuff like
identification, for instance,

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:39.536
when somebody who could be
you wins something for no

00:08:39.536 --> 00:08:42.917
particular good reason, against
incredible odds.

00:08:42.917 --> 00:08:48.604
And is there some sort of
mechanism for you to feel I am

00:08:48.604 --> 00:08:51.176
now happy partly for this
person's success, therefore,

00:08:51.176 --> 00:08:53.740
I'm a winner.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:59.412
Then you get to have that
experience of ecstasy.

00:08:59.412 --> 00:09:02.460
You get to that news.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.116
A couple of videos.

00:09:04.116 --> 00:09:10.113
Some of them it's tongue and
cheek, but hopefully that

00:09:10.113 --> 00:09:11.363
you-- it will kind of illustrate
my point as well.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:34.050
So was anyone here in
Boston in 2004?

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.500
You were in Boston in 2004?

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.438
What happened in
Boston in 2004?

00:09:39.438 --> 00:09:40.794
AUDIENCE: With the Red Sox?

00:09:40.794 --> 00:09:41.246
PROFESSOR: Yes.

00:09:41.246 --> 00:09:46.801
The Red Sox won after, what, 86
years of coming real close

00:09:46.801 --> 00:09:50.575
and never actually winning
a World Series.

00:09:50.575 --> 00:09:55.950
And I'm going to--

00:09:55.950 --> 00:10:00.850
This is a neat little
video that I have.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.310
I just kind of then just use
this as a back drop.

00:10:03.310 --> 00:10:05.906
I think this is right
around Fenwick Hall.

00:10:05.906 --> 00:10:07.394
I'm not so sure it's
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:13.662
Sorry, the wireless in this
particular corner of the

00:10:13.662 --> 00:10:16.840
office can be bad.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:19.750
This is-- actually let
me skip a little bit.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:38.566
So for those of you who don't
know the story, actually how

00:10:38.566 --> 00:10:41.920
many of you don't know what
happened in 2004 around here?

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:42.790
AUDIENCE: Well I don't
know exactly what

00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:44.040
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:54.140
PROFESSOR: So basically what
happened around here was the

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:59.910
Red Sox were down 3, 0
against the Yankees.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.844
And managed to come
back from them.

00:11:02.844 --> 00:11:05.778
And then they beat the Cardinals
and they won the

00:11:05.778 --> 00:11:07.245
World Series.

00:11:07.245 --> 00:11:09.890
Now, this is, of course,
after 86 years, again.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:13.717
I mentioned really people
just not winning at all.

00:11:13.717 --> 00:11:14.700
But coming really close.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.500
Each time they get really,
really close and then they'd

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:20.364
magnificently flub it.

00:11:20.364 --> 00:11:22.300
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:27.990
So this earlier video here is
what Boston looked like when

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:31.410
it was down 3, 0.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:32.660
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:50.015
The funniest thing that every
time the Red Sox actually won

00:11:50.015 --> 00:11:51.660
it was like an awakening.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:57.160
So they weren't at Fenway.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:01.048
When all of these celebrations
that-- can you see once the

00:12:01.048 --> 00:12:02.298
buffering stops.

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:13.150
All the celebration that's going
on is occurring miles

00:12:13.150 --> 00:12:17.346
and miles, probably 100 miles
away from where the game's

00:12:17.346 --> 00:12:21.242
actually happening with
the Cardinals.

00:12:21.242 --> 00:12:23.780
And of course, everyone was
watching it on TV, everyone's

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:27.140
identifying with the Red Sox.

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:30.971
And right now you are sort of
seeing like, as far as I can

00:12:30.971 --> 00:12:33.306
tell probably the Northeastern
students

00:12:33.306 --> 00:12:35.650
who were around Fenway.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:39.160
But in here, the vehicles
driving by and

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:41.120
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:47.000
Everybody was listening on the
radio, you can see the cars

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:47.560
driving by.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:48.649
Everybody knows immediately.

00:12:48.649 --> 00:12:51.144
Everyone in Boston immediately
knew it

00:12:51.144 --> 00:12:52.142
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:12:52.142 --> 00:12:54.637
If they weren't watching
the game, they would

00:12:54.637 --> 00:12:56.134
have heard it anyway.

00:12:56.134 --> 00:12:59.128
Because they have no
affirmative claims.

00:12:59.128 --> 00:13:02.621
The only way that you would
cheer the Red Sox is to say

00:13:02.621 --> 00:13:05.116
Mackie sucked, which is
kind of sad actually.

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:10.930
For those of you who weren't
around, it was a really,

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:12.680
really awesome night.

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:14.680
There was, of course, a little
bit of violence.

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:18.110
Things got a little bit
out-- a bunch of

00:13:18.110 --> 00:13:19.360
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:13:23.335 --> 00:13:27.480
But it wasn't like a huge
riot or anything.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.513
For the most part it was
pretty much this.

00:13:29.513 --> 00:13:32.340
A lot of people with a lot
of the hands in the air.

00:13:32.340 --> 00:13:34.781
No cars being turned over
or anything like that.

00:13:34.781 --> 00:13:36.260
But it was being--

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:42.170
But these are the kind of
aesthetic movements that

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:42.950
[? Colera ?]

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:45.898
talks about in his write-up.

00:13:45.898 --> 00:13:46.390
Yup?

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:49.342
AUDIENCE: I have a video on my
phone in Barcelona right after

00:13:49.342 --> 00:13:53.278
they won the semi-finals
over the summer.

00:13:53.278 --> 00:13:57.214
And I had a video of the
streets at 11 PM.

00:13:57.214 --> 00:14:00.166
I could show you
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:14:00.166 --> 00:14:02.134
I have a video of the
streets at 12:30,

00:14:02.134 --> 00:14:03.610
so half past midnight.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:05.578
And it's the exact same scene.

00:14:05.578 --> 00:14:08.530
And the exact same people are
still in the street with their

00:14:08.530 --> 00:14:10.990
shirts off, still twirling
their shirts around.

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:13.942
