00:41:11.242 --> 00:41:17.098
This is the important part of
this model is that this is

00:41:17.098 --> 00:41:20.782
where noise gets
in the channel.

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:27.744
The reason why this model is
useful for thinking about

00:41:27.744 --> 00:41:30.670
games is you can actually
think of the

00:41:30.670 --> 00:41:32.980
sender as the game.

00:41:32.980 --> 00:41:34.680
This this game space.

00:41:40.630 --> 00:41:44.530
And the message could be
something, it could be

00:41:44.530 --> 00:41:49.665
valuable information about the
current state of the game in

00:41:49.665 --> 00:41:50.915
order for you to make
a good decision.

00:41:53.622 --> 00:41:57.410
In poker, it will be, well
everybody's hands.

00:41:57.410 --> 00:41:59.830
That's the message.

00:41:59.830 --> 00:42:01.345
That's valuable information.

00:42:01.345 --> 00:42:04.321
If you knew what everybody had
in your hand, and if you knew

00:42:04.321 --> 00:42:08.070
everything in the deck in the
order in which the deck is

00:42:08.070 --> 00:42:11.520
shuffled, you will be able to
make a great decision about

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:16.220
whether to raise your
bid on the floor.

00:42:19.130 --> 00:42:23.000
And so you have rules that
sort of encode what that

00:42:23.000 --> 00:42:27.225
message is going to be, and
you have rules that--

00:42:27.225 --> 00:42:31.135
you have strategies to help you
figure out what to do with

00:42:31.135 --> 00:42:35.300
that, with the receiver kind of
becomes and you think of it

00:42:35.300 --> 00:42:37.260
as [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:42:37.260 --> 00:42:38.730
as a player's position,
actually.

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:43.874
Or the player's strategy if you
would think about it over

00:42:43.874 --> 00:42:45.124
a long-term strategy.

00:42:48.510 --> 00:42:49.850
The trick is noise.

00:42:49.850 --> 00:42:52.390
And noise is actually something
that you get to

00:42:52.390 --> 00:42:54.590
control [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:42:54.590 --> 00:42:55.410
What is hidden?

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:57.880
What is obfiscated?

00:42:57.880 --> 00:43:01.934
What can be deliberately
introduced to confuse you

00:43:01.934 --> 00:43:04.294
about what the game
state actually is?

00:43:07.630 --> 00:43:12.042
Noise can be random, or noise
can be just plain old you

00:43:12.042 --> 00:43:13.540
don't know.

00:43:13.540 --> 00:43:17.370
Just deleting information out
of the [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:43:17.370 --> 00:43:22.380
So for instance, you guys know
the rules of Texas Bulldog?

00:43:22.380 --> 00:43:26.235
There is a set of cards in the
middle called the river.

00:43:26.235 --> 00:43:28.640
I could be wrong, so if you
really know the rules

00:43:28.640 --> 00:43:30.570
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:43:30.570 --> 00:43:34.793
And these cards are dealt pretty
early in the game.

00:43:34.793 --> 00:43:38.174
They're not dealt right at
the beginning, but the--

00:43:38.174 --> 00:43:41.555
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
at the beginning?

00:43:41.555 --> 00:43:43.970
AUDIENCE: Not all at once
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:43:43.970 --> 00:43:44.936
AUDIENCE: The first three.

00:43:44.936 --> 00:43:46.868
AUDIENCE: The flop, yeah.

00:43:46.868 --> 00:43:47.860
And then [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:43:47.860 --> 00:43:49.620
PROFESSOR: Oh, the flop
and the river, right.

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:51.090
Which is the one
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]?

00:43:51.090 --> 00:43:51.875
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:43:51.875 --> 00:43:52.925
PROFESSOR: The flop is data?

00:43:52.925 --> 00:43:54.175
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:43:57.760 --> 00:43:59.720
AUDIENCE: Everyone gets dealt
the two cards, and then you

00:43:59.720 --> 00:44:02.950
turn the flop over, which
[INTERPOSING VOICES].

00:44:02.950 --> 00:44:04.070
And then the river is the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:44:04.070 --> 00:44:05.310
PROFESSOR: The river's
the big one.

00:44:05.310 --> 00:44:07.625
AUDIENCE: And betting
zero, yeah.

00:44:07.625 --> 00:44:10.155
AUDIENCE: Well no, technically
when you deal you put one card

00:44:10.155 --> 00:44:12.576
face down then you put the
flop [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:44:12.576 --> 00:44:13.995
AUDIENCE: That part doesn't
have anything to do

00:44:13.995 --> 00:44:14.468
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:44:14.468 --> 00:44:15.890
AUDIENCE: Yeah, but I
don't know what--

00:44:15.890 --> 00:44:17.961
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:44:17.961 --> 00:44:20.300
But the cards would
get dealt out.

00:44:20.300 --> 00:44:25.162
It's pretty much an unknown.

00:44:25.162 --> 00:44:26.540
It is random.

00:44:26.540 --> 00:44:29.700
But on and above this, if you
just put five cards in

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:32.770
whatever side and then
just dealt them out.

00:44:32.770 --> 00:44:34.430
It's a fixed quantity.

00:44:34.430 --> 00:44:37.880
But you still don't know
what the heck it is.

00:44:37.880 --> 00:44:40.135
Someone else could have picked
those cards and still don't

00:44:40.135 --> 00:44:41.154
know what it is.

00:44:41.154 --> 00:44:44.450
So they're effectively
a random thing.

00:44:44.450 --> 00:44:45.450
They're noise.

00:44:45.450 --> 00:44:47.694
They're a bit of information
we don't know.

00:44:47.694 --> 00:44:50.930
And in Texas Hold'em, a bit of
information that everybody

00:44:50.930 --> 00:44:53.405
does know, so that's great.

00:44:53.405 --> 00:44:56.622
The information is kind of
equally shared among everybody

00:44:56.622 --> 00:44:57.860
except for the one card.

00:44:57.860 --> 00:44:59.110
They'd be fine.

00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:03.360
So--

00:45:03.360 --> 00:45:07.800
--when you think about how you
can even things out for the

00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:12.883
players, or how you can make
the challenge a little bit

00:45:12.883 --> 00:45:17.090
higher for decent players, you
can think about, well how much

00:45:17.090 --> 00:45:19.422
information am I currently
giving them about what

00:45:19.422 --> 00:45:22.000
a game state is?

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:25.595
And maybe I can, I'll just
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] them.

00:45:25.595 --> 00:45:28.073
Not necessarily lie,
just hide.

00:45:28.073 --> 00:45:30.911
Just deny them information
that is necessarily

00:45:30.911 --> 00:45:32.330
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:45:32.330 --> 00:45:35.250
It's also a good way to sort
of think of well maybe if

00:45:35.250 --> 00:45:38.300
you're a little bit behind and
you need a little help, then

00:45:38.300 --> 00:45:40.370
what are you going to do
to give them a fighting

00:45:40.370 --> 00:45:41.348
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] channel.

00:45:41.348 --> 00:45:44.771
Let them have the next card
that's coming up.

00:45:44.771 --> 00:45:46.727
Because we're talking about
card games here.

00:45:46.727 --> 00:45:50.150
Let them re-roll the
next die roll.

00:45:50.150 --> 00:45:51.400
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:45:55.690 --> 00:46:00.130
This model, about like
thinking what sort of

00:46:00.130 --> 00:46:03.465
information are you giving your
player can also sort of

00:46:03.465 --> 00:46:06.030
be useful in thinking whether
they're giving too much

00:46:06.030 --> 00:46:08.376
information strictly from a
decision-making point of view.

00:46:08.376 --> 00:46:11.170
It's like I'm throwing all this
game state information,

00:46:11.170 --> 00:46:16.980
but really, all the player needs
to know is half of that

00:46:16.980 --> 00:46:19.820
in order to make a
good decision.

00:46:19.820 --> 00:46:21.943
You might actually
want to consider

00:46:21.943 --> 00:46:24.841
reducing that somehow.

00:46:24.841 --> 00:46:27.739
Let me think of an example.

00:46:27.739 --> 00:46:31.844
I think Settlers of Catan that
should be a pretty good

00:46:31.844 --> 00:46:35.040
example of that.

00:46:35.040 --> 00:46:35.940
You can see the entire board.

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:40.100
Everyone can see the entire
board at any given time.

00:46:40.100 --> 00:46:43.520
And everyone's got cards
in their hands.

00:46:43.520 --> 00:46:45.630
You don't know what other people
have in their cards.

00:46:45.630 --> 00:46:48.570
But for the most part, when
you're trying to make a

00:46:48.570 --> 00:46:52.170
decision about what to do with
your cards, you're really just

00:46:52.170 --> 00:46:55.586
concerned about your cards.

00:46:55.586 --> 00:46:58.514
As time goes on, you pretty much
know what other people

00:46:58.514 --> 00:47:00.880
have in their hand if you've
been paying attention, because

00:47:00.880 --> 00:47:03.900
people are picking up resources
fairly openly.

00:47:06.620 --> 00:47:08.940
But you don't really care.

00:47:08.940 --> 00:47:11.665
And that's one nice thing about
Settlers is that they're

00:47:11.665 --> 00:47:13.442
hiding this information from
you, even though it is

00:47:13.442 --> 00:47:14.575
information that need
to be hidden.

00:47:14.575 --> 00:47:17.970
Because in order to make a good
decision in that game,

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:19.920
you do not need to know
that information.

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:23.640
So they're introducting noise,
they're deliberately hiding

00:47:23.640 --> 00:47:27.620
information from you so that you
can just focus on the two

00:47:27.620 --> 00:47:29.837
or three decisions that
are actually worth

00:47:29.837 --> 00:47:31.864
making on your turn.

00:47:31.864 --> 00:47:35.036
And that's one reason why
Settlers games actually go

00:47:35.036 --> 00:47:38.208
pretty fast because people
aren't paralyzed by too much

00:47:38.208 --> 00:47:40.650
information.

00:47:40.650 --> 00:47:41.970
If your game's getting
too long.

00:47:41.970 --> 00:47:45.190
The game's supposed to be a half
an hour and it's taking

00:47:45.190 --> 00:47:47.690
an hour long, think about the
information that you're giving

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:48.660
your player.

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:51.270
All of it might be relevant to
the game state, but not all of

00:47:51.270 --> 00:47:54.001
it might be relevant to what the
players need to know right

00:47:54.001 --> 00:47:58.098
now in order to make
their next move.

00:47:58.098 --> 00:48:02.110
And if you find a way to control
that, sometimes just a

00:48:02.110 --> 00:48:04.690
clever color coding.

00:48:04.690 --> 00:48:07.650
Making your card in such a way
that it's really, really

00:48:07.650 --> 00:48:10.023
obvious that there's a certain
part of the card that you need

00:48:10.023 --> 00:48:13.290
to look at at certain times,
and you kind of ignore it

00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:16.965
outside on a [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
call other times, and that

00:48:16.965 --> 00:48:18.680
might help.

00:48:18.680 --> 00:48:24.110
But often what I find is
in a lot of games like

00:48:24.110 --> 00:48:27.695
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] card games,
for instance, board games,

00:48:27.695 --> 00:48:32.580
they would use a lot of icons to
describe what the value of

00:48:32.580 --> 00:48:34.830
something is, or what kind
of effect it has.

00:48:34.830 --> 00:48:36.870
And the reason why they use
icons instead of using words

00:48:36.870 --> 00:48:39.270
is because you don't have
translate icons.

00:48:39.270 --> 00:48:42.487
And they want their games to
sell in Germany, in Spain, in

00:48:42.487 --> 00:48:48.500
England, and US, and Greece, and
Italy, and so if you made

00:48:48.500 --> 00:48:51.843
cards, you couldn't use those
same cards in every one of of

00:48:51.843 --> 00:48:52.660
those countries, so
you use icons.

00:48:52.660 --> 00:48:55.190
The only problem is that now you
have to learn what every

00:48:55.190 --> 00:48:56.854
one of those icons mean.

00:48:56.854 --> 00:48:59.480
If it's just a stack of coins,
oh 5 coins means 5?

00:48:59.480 --> 00:49:01.345
OK, all right, I can probably
figure that out.

00:49:01.345 --> 00:49:02.250
That's fine.

00:49:02.250 --> 00:49:05.928
But this is a picture of a
hammer, what does a hammer do?

00:49:05.928 --> 00:49:07.650
I'll have to check in the
reference manual, which

00:49:07.650 --> 00:49:08.900
happens to be in six different
languages.

00:49:11.780 --> 00:49:15.448
And that's one of the barriers
that a lot of folks have to

00:49:15.448 --> 00:49:16.412
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:49:16.412 --> 00:49:18.178
in card games and board games.

00:49:18.178 --> 00:49:20.260
It's not necessarily that
the games are all that

00:49:20.260 --> 00:49:20.758
complicated.

00:49:20.758 --> 00:49:22.999
It's just that you have to
actually have translation that

00:49:22.999 --> 00:49:24.021
has to be done because
they're not really

00:49:24.021 --> 00:49:25.271
built for one language.

00:49:30.220 --> 00:49:36.196
So I probably have more to say
about information, but I'm

00:49:36.196 --> 00:49:38.686
going to save it till
next class.

00:49:38.686 --> 00:49:39.936
Any questions so far?

00:49:43.666 --> 00:49:45.907
Everything that I'm sort of
directing at you right now are

00:49:45.907 --> 00:49:48.646
pretty much just different
ways to look at the game

00:49:48.646 --> 00:49:49.642
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:49:49.642 --> 00:49:53.840
It's a card game, but try to
see it from different ways.

00:49:53.840 --> 00:49:57.740
Not just how do I get this one
game mechanic to work.

00:49:57.740 --> 00:50:00.220
But what are people going to be
getting out of this game.

00:50:00.220 --> 00:50:01.950
When they're going to be drawing
this big card, how are

00:50:01.950 --> 00:50:03.450
they going to be feeling?

00:50:03.450 --> 00:50:05.290
Are they going to be
getting excited?

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:07.600
Apprehensive?

00:50:07.600 --> 00:50:10.845
Are they going to be counting
on the next card to win them

00:50:10.845 --> 00:50:12.545
the game, or is this just
going to be giving them

00:50:12.545 --> 00:50:15.613
options, and do they know
what options they have?

00:50:19.064 --> 00:50:42.728
So I'm just going
to do a complete

00:50:42.728 --> 00:50:44.207
transition at this point.

00:50:44.207 --> 00:50:45.686
So new topic.

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:55.110
One thing I want to talk about,
which is not actually

00:50:55.110 --> 00:50:57.400
covered in any of the
readings is the body

00:50:57.400 --> 00:50:58.720
motion think plate.

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:00.760
I think it's probably
used as, sort of--

00:51:00.760 --> 00:51:03.732
Jason and I use this
story quite a bit.

00:51:03.732 --> 00:51:06.806
We haven't really designed
it all that well.

00:51:06.806 --> 00:51:13.480
But if you think of the
definition of what a good game

00:51:13.480 --> 00:51:14.750
could be, [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:51:14.750 --> 00:51:18.240
offers, a good game is a series
of interesting choices.

00:51:18.240 --> 00:51:21.240
Our answer is all interesting
decisions.

00:51:21.240 --> 00:51:26.640
So what's an interesting
decision?

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:30.560
One argument is that an
interesting decision is one

00:51:30.560 --> 00:51:35.325
where you're using mechanics
to affect the game state.

00:51:35.325 --> 00:51:40.050
And you're going to get some
information, you're going to

00:51:40.050 --> 00:51:44.240
use that mechanic and effect
the game state.

00:51:44.240 --> 00:51:46.900
The game state, the game's going
to respond in some way.

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:50.260
And how that decision you make
corresponds to the result that

00:51:50.260 --> 00:51:54.120
you get back from the game state
as could be interesting.

00:51:54.120 --> 00:51:56.270
If there's no correspondence
at all, if it's completely

00:51:56.270 --> 00:52:02.236
random or, even worse,
completely predictable, than

00:52:02.236 --> 00:52:05.475
these are not interesting
options that you've got.

00:52:05.475 --> 00:52:06.670
And [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:52:06.670 --> 00:52:08.450
huh.

00:52:08.450 --> 00:52:12.759
That result logically made
sense, but I didn't expect

00:52:12.759 --> 00:52:14.737
that it was going to do
it, or that turned out

00:52:14.737 --> 00:52:16.116
better than I expected.

00:52:16.116 --> 00:52:17.366
Now it becomes really
interesting.

00:52:19.855 --> 00:52:23.580
And if it's just a--

00:52:23.580 --> 00:52:24.165
it could be anything.

00:52:24.165 --> 00:52:24.950
It could be awesome.

00:52:24.950 --> 00:52:26.950
I could win this game
with this next--

00:52:26.950 --> 00:52:29.340
oh, look I won.

00:52:29.340 --> 00:52:30.590
That's not interesting either.

00:52:33.280 --> 00:52:37.270
So if that's an interesting
decision, [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:52:37.270 --> 00:52:42.360
calls it a meaningful decision,
then the converse

00:52:42.360 --> 00:52:44.150
will be a meaningless
decision.

00:52:44.150 --> 00:52:48.785
So like a game like what
I just described.

00:52:48.785 --> 00:52:51.530
A decision where making a choice
doesn't actually affect

00:52:51.530 --> 00:52:52.400
your outcome.

00:52:52.400 --> 00:52:53.350
Because [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:52:53.350 --> 00:52:57.028
makes card flip, or you had to
do the card flip anyway.

00:52:57.028 --> 00:52:58.240
You can't choose.

00:52:58.240 --> 00:53:02.250
Then that's not a meaningful
decision.

00:53:02.250 --> 00:53:05.000
Or you make a decision and have
no change in the game

00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:06.431
state at all.

00:53:06.431 --> 00:53:08.340
That's not interesting either.

00:53:08.340 --> 00:53:16.630
So what you want in your game is
something where you've got

00:53:16.630 --> 00:53:20.190
a system that's nonlinear,
that's one that's still

00:53:20.190 --> 00:53:22.350
deterministic.

00:53:22.350 --> 00:53:27.870
All of your rules will result
in some sort of outcome.

00:53:27.870 --> 00:53:31.140
But nonlinear enough that
you can't really sort of

00:53:31.140 --> 00:53:35.380
anticipate the entire
possibility space when you're

00:53:35.380 --> 00:53:39.350
making that decision.

00:53:39.350 --> 00:53:41.640
If you're thinking of games,
that's a huge possibility

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:44.230
space, then you, as the
designer, your job is to try

00:53:44.230 --> 00:53:49.620
to make them most frazzled areas
of possibility space

00:53:49.620 --> 00:53:51.700
interesting and fun.

00:53:51.700 --> 00:53:56.816
So actually do folks know the
phrase possibility space.

00:53:56.816 --> 00:54:02.690
Yeah, and if you think of a game
as just a series of terms

00:54:02.690 --> 00:54:04.540
or series of decisions.

00:54:04.540 --> 00:54:08.340
Then possibility space is
basically every single result

00:54:08.340 --> 00:54:11.290
that would come out from
every single legal

00:54:11.290 --> 00:54:13.340
decision in your game.

00:54:13.340 --> 00:54:16.350
Some of them are going
to make sense.

00:54:16.350 --> 00:54:18.190
Like--

00:54:18.190 --> 00:54:20.960
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:54:20.960 --> 00:54:22.520
PROFESSOR: In process, which
now I'm getting back to the

00:54:22.520 --> 00:54:24.782
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:54:24.782 --> 00:54:31.650
Someone's got zero and zero,
and it's my turn.

00:54:31.650 --> 00:54:34.030
Putting in actually
what makes sense.

00:54:34.030 --> 00:54:38.200
Putting it anywhere else
doesn't make any sense.

00:54:38.200 --> 00:54:42.720
So I would argue that putting
an X anywhere really isn't--

00:54:42.720 --> 00:54:45.939
well it is part of the
possibility space, because I

00:54:45.939 --> 00:54:48.660
could put it here, here,
here, here or here.

00:54:48.660 --> 00:54:52.400
You as a game designer, your job
is to make sure that the

00:54:52.400 --> 00:54:57.644
things that people won't
naturally do are interesting.

00:54:57.644 --> 00:55:01.430
You do not have necessarily
any responsibility for

00:55:01.430 --> 00:55:07.340
somebody who's just trying to
play your game in a way that's

00:55:07.340 --> 00:55:10.770
